\p{\strong{Tests.} \code{\verb~|work/exports/all~}: the arena's complete test set (the 230 files of the published tarball, the two \code{\verb~|perf/magma-string-*~} tests and the eight corpora that the tarball leaves out, the 30 \code{\verb~|either~} tests; 270 files, byte-identical to the arena's by \code{\verb~|manifest.json~}), with the arena's outcome and time limit per test. Tests without an arena limit ran with 1,800 s.}

\p{\strong{Procedure}, per configuration (\code{\verb~|work/ar1/scripts/suite.sh~}):}

\pre{\verb~|
bench/slot.sh -- bench/arena-docker.sh --arena-limits --no-network \
    bench/run-suite.py --perf --arena-timeouts --timeout 1800 --tests work/exports/all \
    --cwd work/ar1/src --report work/ar1/reports/LABEL.json -v -- 'RUN LINE'
~}

\p{\code{\verb~|work/ar1/src/work/dist~} is a link to \code{\verb~|work/ar1/dist~}, so the run line of \code{\verb~|rlvm.yaml~} is executed verbatim with the checker's checkout as working directory, through \code{\verb~|sh -c~}, with \code{\verb~|$IN~} and \code{\verb~|LC_ALL=C~}, under \code{\verb~|perf stat -e instructions:u~} (all threads and children) and GNU time, tests strictly one after the other. Mathlib was run a second time per configuration in a container of its own inside a \src{bench/quiet.py} window (\code{\verb~|work/ar1/scripts/one.sh --quiet~}); so were the leaders (\code{\verb~|work/ar1/scripts/leaders.sh~}) and the one test that timed out.}

\p{Run lines:}

\ul{
\li{(a) \code{\verb~|RLVM_STATUS_BASE=10 work/dist/bin/rlvm --threads 4 "$IN"; case $? in 10) exit 0;; 11) exit 1;; 12) exit 2;; *) exit 3;; esac~} (the \code{\verb~|run:~} line of \code{\verb~|rlvm.yaml~}, sha256 of the file \code{\verb~|390d58c2a289...~})}
\li{(b) the same with \code{\verb~|-X engine=machine~} after \code{\verb~|--threads 4~}}
\li{(c*) (b) plus \code{\verb~|-X machine.switches=gated,pchk,expect,warm,code,casetrees,bodies,directrules,same,match,joint,lazycases,lazyeval~}}
\li{(d) (b) plus \code{\verb~|-X machine.switches=default,facts,probes -Drlvm.machine.m4=congruence,skipargs~}}
}

\p{Times (UTC): suite (a) 18:36:52 - 18:52:12, (b) 18:52:21 - 19:01:15, (d) 19:07:26 - 19:13:51, (c*) 19:22:00 - 19:26:40 (it waited for a host slot from 19:14:29); quiet windows: Mathlib (a) ended 18:49:17, (b) 18:58:41, the three Rust leaders 19:04:02, \code{\verb~|loop-unfolded-once~} 19:09:43, rpylean about 19:18, (c*) 19:21:46.}

\p{\strong{A caveat that turned out not to matter.} The host was busy (load average 35 - 60 on 20 cores) and the quiet lock was taken back to back by other agents, so my suite containers spent most of their time parked on the two spare performance cores (the checker's own statistics show \code{\verb~|processors=2~}). Instruction counts of the parked runs agree with the quiet-window runs to -0.22 \% (a), +0.07 \% (b) and -0.02 \% (d); wall and CPU of the suite runs are not evidence, and one arena time limit was hit only because of the parking (\code{\verb~|perf/loop-unfolded-once~}, 0.1).}
}

\subtree{
\title{2. Verdicts in detail}

\p{\code{\verb~|either~} tests (free in the ranking; listed because they show the policy): (a), (b) and (c*) give the same 28 verdicts - 9 accepted (\code{\verb~|alg-conv-trans-acc-left~}, \code{\verb~|-acc-right~}, \code{\verb~|alg-conv-trans-quot-left-def~}, \code{\verb~|-quot-right~}, \code{\verb~|nested-redex-aux~}, \code{\verb~|proj-maybe-prop~}, \code{\verb~|proj-maybe-prop-past~}, \code{\verb~|subject-reduction-redex~}, \code{\verb~|vanishing-occurrence~}) and 19 rejected, among them \code{\verb~|imax-right-successor~} and \code{\verb~|imax-nested-absorption~} (owner note 11) - and decline the two non-terminating files. That is the published \code{\verb~|official~} verdict on every one of the 28. (d) accepts two more, \code{\verb~|alg-conv-trans-quot-left~} and \code{\verb~|eta-struct-major-and~}: the verdicts of gruszka, sokonanoda and rpylean on those files.}

\p{Against the published results of others, configuration (a): \code{\verb~|official~} (v4.35.0) differs on 2 tests (the non-terminating pair: timeout there, decline here); \code{\verb~|official-nightly~} on 9 \code{\verb~|either~} tests; nano-lean on 4 (its decline of \code{\verb~|bugs/nat-shift-huge~}, which RLVM accepts, and 3 \code{\verb~|either~}); gruszka on 4 \code{\verb~|either~}; sokonanoda on 11; rpylean on 8. Declines, errors and timeouts of the leaders on the published board: nano-lean 3, gruszka 0, sokonanoda 6, rpylean 4, pestka 0, kefir 0, palladium 6.}
}

