\p{All eight corpora are accepted in every configuration, far inside the arena's limits (the largest share of a limit: Mathlib (a), 177.7 s of 4,120 s in the quiet window). Against (b), the candidate set (c*) saves 71 \% on reachability, 28 \% on init, 25 \% on con-leche, 24 \% on std, 21 \% on cedar, 15 \% on navier-stokes-euler, 12 \% on cslib and 7.7 \% on Mathlib: the corpus the arena ranks by is the one it helps least (P8: Mathlib's extra cost is the conversion of proofs, which no official-exact switch touches). The shortcuts of (d) are worth 33.6 \% on Mathlib, 55.9 \% on navier-stokes-euler, 19.1 \% on cslib and 0 - 5 \% on the other five corpora.}

\p{Small tests with the largest counts (instructions; wall of the parked suite run in brackets is NOT evidence): \code{\verb~|perf/magma-list-deep-n36~} (limit 360 s) (a) 181.0 G, (b) 60.8 G; \code{\verb~|perf/magma-list-pair-n21~} (240 s) 115.4 G, 50.2 G; \code{\verb~|perf/ite-taken-branch~} (230 s) 109.6 G, 39.9 G; \code{\verb~|perf/gf3-matrix-product~} (70 s) 97.5 G, 19.4 G; \code{\verb~|perf/app-lam~} (70 s) 55.3 G, 0.8 G; the four \code{\verb~|*-bad~} perf tests 52 - 72 G in (a) and 54 - 62 G in (b) (a rejection is confirmed by the reference engine in either configuration). Peak RSS of a small test: 4,042 MiB (\code{\verb~|perf/app-lam~}, (a)); with the machine 3,722 MiB (\code{\verb~|perf/magma-list-deep-n36~}) apart from the 8,686 MiB of \code{\verb~|perf/loop-unfolded-once~}.}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. The leaders, same container, same session}

\p{Table 0.2. The local builds are those of \code{\verb~|bench/baselines/~} (arena revision \code{\verb~|99195b6b~}); each ran through its generated wrapper (\code{\verb~|bench/baselines/NAME.sh~}, the arena's run line) in a container of its own inside a quiet window (gruszka, nano-lean, sokonanoda in one window, rpylean in another). Their counts repeat this morning's (361.3 / 418.7 / 504.3 / 544.6 G) to 0.02 - 0.26 \%. Quiet wall clock with their 4 threads: gruszka 20.0 s, nano-lean 29.5 s, sokonanoda 33.7 s; rpylean (one thread) 125.8 s. RLVM with 4 workers, same windows: (a) 177.7 s, (b) 61.3 s, (c*) 56.8 s - 3.1 times gruszka and 2.1 times nano-lean for (b), a much smaller distance than in instructions (7.1 and 8.2 times), because the machine retires about 14.5 G instructions per CPU second against gruszka's 5.9 and nano-lean's 3.5. (Cold process, batch load, 4 workers: the arena's configuration, not the owner's; the owner's table is \code{\verb~|work/status/warm.md~}.)}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. The projection}

\p{\code{\verb~|place~} = 1 + the number of published checkers whose key (bad accepted, good rejected, Mathlib instructions, declined) sorts before RLVM's (\code{\verb~|work/ar1/board.py~}, \code{\verb~|work/ar1/scripts/mathlib-table.py~}: the arena's \code{\verb~|compute_checker_stats~} and \code{\verb~|sort_key~} recomputed from \code{\verb~|results.json~}; they reproduce the published order). RLVM's x86-64 count is unknown; the columns of table 0.3 divide the count of this host by a ratio (count here / published count) measured in this session for a leader, and by the two extremes any baseline has shown. A native image of Java code is neither a Rust binary nor a JVM, so the truth can lie anywhere in that range; the places of (a), (b) and (d) do not depend on where.}

\p{Same-machine cross-check without any ratio: (a) at 8,427 G lies between nanoda (5,533 G here, 12th) and tenet-jvm with C2 (9,313 G here, 15th); (b) at 2,979 G and (d) at 1,977 G lie between pestka (1,340 G here, 5th) and nanoda. kefir, palladium, con-ron (6th to 8th) are not built here, which is where the ratio is needed.}
}

